Here’s how I got it working:

1. Don’t use the `modal` prop in the `Dropdown.Root` component.
2. For the `Dropdown.Content`, add a `container` prop pointing to the `Dialog.Content` element. You can achieve this by using `useRef` to reference the `DialogContent`.

Here’s an example:

```typecript
import React, { useRef } from "react";

import * as Dialog from "@radix-ui/react-dialog";
import * as DropdownMenu from "@radix-ui/react-dropdown-menu";

export function DropdownWithinDialog() {
  const dialogContentRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement | null>(null);

  return (
    <Dialog.Root>
      <Dialog.Trigger>Open Dialog</Dialog.Trigger>
      <Dialog.Portal>
        {/* 👇 Attach the ref to Dialog.Content */}
        <Dialog.Content ref={dialogContentRef}>
          <div>
            {/* 👇 Avoid adding the `modal` prop to DropdownMenu.Root */}
            <DropdownMenu.Root>
              <DropdownMenu.Trigger>Open Dropdown</DropdownMenu.Trigger>
              <DropdownMenu.Portal>
                {/* 👇 Set the container to the dialog content element */}
                <DropdownMenu.Content
                  container={dialogContentRef.current || undefined}
                >
                  Hello World!
                </DropdownMenu.Content>
              </DropdownMenu.Portal>
            </DropdownMenu.Root>
          </div>
        </Dialog.Content>
      </Dialog.Portal>
    </Dialog.Root>
  );
}
```

Key Points:
- The `container` prop in `DropdownMenu.Content` ensures the dropdown stays inside the dialog.
- Use `useRef` to grab a reference to the dialog’s `Content` and pass it down.

Hope this helps!

